    "['interface [interface_name] passive', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ospf 1', 'parent_command': ['ospf 1'], 'root': 'ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0\\n##\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei devices, OSPF is configured with a process ID (1 in this case)\\n2. The area is specified (0.0.0.0 matching the Juniper config)\\n3. Instead of a \"passive\" flag for loopback, we advertise the specific loopback address (4.4.4.4/32) with a 0.0.0.0 wildcard mask\\n4. Note that Huawei doesn\\'t have an exact \"passive interface\" concept for OSPF like Juniper - loopback interfaces are automatically treated as passive in Huawei OSPF implementations', 'trans_template': 'ospf 1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'area 0.0.0.0', 'parent_command': ['ospf 1'], 'root': 'ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0\\n##\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei devices, OSPF is configured with a process ID (1 in this case)\\n2. The area is specified (0.0.0.0 matching the Juniper config)\\n3. Instead of a \"passive\" flag for loopback, we advertise the specific loopback address (4.4.4.4/32) with a 0.0.0.0 wildcard mask\\n4. Note that Huawei doesn\\'t have an exact \"passive interface\" concept for OSPF like Juniper - loopback interfaces are automatically treated as passive in Huawei OSPF implementations', 'trans_template': 'area 0.0.0.0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0', 'parent_command': ['ospf 1'], 'root': 'ospf 1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nospf 1\\n area 0.0.0.0\\n  network 4.4.4.4 0.0.0.0\\n##\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei devices, OSPF is configured with a process ID (1 in this case)\\n2. The area is specified (0.0.0.0 matching the Juniper config)\\n3. Instead of a \"passive\" flag for loopback, we advertise the specific loopback address (4.4.4.4/32) with a 0.0.0.0 wildcard mask\\n4. Note that Huawei doesn\\'t have an exact